You are auditing a post-publish fixup pass of the Harn release flow. The
release artifact for `{{ target_version }}` has already shipped:
`{{ fixup_tag }}` was pushed during the original `publish-release.yml` run,
and crates.io + the GitHub release are live for that exact commit. The
open Release PR (`{{ fixup_pr_url }}`) is paperwork — its job is to land
the Cargo.toml/CHANGELOG bump on `{{ base_branch }}`, not to influence
what was published.

Constraints — these are non-negotiable, even if the fact sheet seems to
imply otherwise:

- The `{{ fixup_tag }}` tag must stay where it is. Do not recommend
  retagging, force-pushing, or moving it. Do not recommend running
  `publish-release.yml` again. Re-shipping a published version is
  impossible (crates.io is immutable) and would diverge artifacts.
- The release branch was force-pushed onto a fresh `{{ base_branch }}`
  snapshot. Conflicts you may have seen on the open PR before this run
  no longer exist on the harness side; the open PR will pick up the new
  branch state automatically.
- The `## Unreleased` content that landed on `{{ base_branch }}` after
  `{{ fixup_tag }}` was pushed has been deterministically folded into
  the shipped v{{ target_version }} body by the harness. Do not author
  a new release section.

Your job is a tight verification pass. Use only local read-only tools.
Inspect the merged CHANGELOG.md and at least one PR/diff/file source
that the post-tag drift touched. Then return a short Audit block:

- Confirm the merged v{{ target_version }} body still reads as a release
  for that version (the folded entries describe behavior that genuinely
  belongs in `{{ target_version }}` rather than a future version).
- Flag any folded entries that look misclassified (would belong in a
  later release) or duplicated.
- Confirm the harness left the tag and published artifact untouched.

If everything looks right, say so explicitly. Do not author a draft
changelog. Do not include `BEGIN_DRAFT_CHANGELOG` markers.
